---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site';
---

<Page path="/how-it-works/">
  <h1>How KVGrainy fits an image under a size limit</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    You pick a size. KVGrainy looks for the version of your image that looks most like the original while staying under it.
    There is no single setting that does that, so it searches.
  </p>

  <h2>What gets tried</h2>
  <p>
    An image gets smaller in two ways: by keeping fewer pixels (a smaller picture) or by describing the pixels less exactly (lower
    quality). Which mix looks best depends on the picture. A photo of a face tolerates a lot of quality loss; a screenshot of
    text does not, but it shrinks well when it has few colours. So for each format KVGrainy:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Starts at the original size and finds the highest quality that fits under your limit.</li>
    <li>
      If that quality is poor, steps the picture down (95%, 90%, 85%, and so on to 30% of the original width and height) and
      repeats, because a smaller picture at higher quality often looks better than a full-size one at low quality.
    </li>
    <li>Stops once making the picture smaller no longer helps.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    It does this for <strong>JPEG, WebP, AVIF and PNG</strong> (JPEG is skipped when your image has transparency, because JPEG
    cannot hold any). PNG has two forms: lossless, which never changes a pixel, and a reduced-colour form that suits flat graphics
    and screenshots; the browser version of this page only has the lossless one.
  </p>

  <h2>How it decides which looks best</h2>
  <p>
    Every candidate is compared to your original with <strong>SSIMULACRA2</strong>, a published perceptual metric designed to
    agree with what people notice: blocky edges, banding, smeared detail and colour shifts. It is far better at this than a plain
    pixel difference, which treats a harmless shift in brightness as badly as a missing eyebrow.
  </p>
  <p>
    The comparison is done on a copy of the original no larger than one megapixel. That keeps it fast and light on memory (a
    12-megapixel photo would otherwise need about two gigabytes to score in a browser), and in our tests it picked the same
    winner as scoring the full image in 99% of cases. The highest score wins; scores that are practically equal go to the larger
    picture, then the larger file, and a result that is visually identical to the original goes to the smaller file.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Quick and Best.</strong> AVIF often gives the smallest file, but it is by far the slowest format to encode. Quick
      skips it and usually finishes several times faster, at the cost of a slightly lower score on some photos. Best tries it. If
      you force a format yourself, that format is always used.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What happens to your image</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It is decoded by your browser, with its orientation (rotate-on-open) applied and its colours converted to the standard sRGB
      space, so the result looks the same on every screen.
    </li>
    <li>
      Everything after that happens in this tab, in background workers: nothing is sent anywhere. See
      <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The output is a fresh encode from pixels, so camera metadata (EXIF, GPS location, the thumbnail) is not carried over.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>GIFs and short clips</h2>
  <p>
    Animations have three knobs instead of two: the picture size, the number of colours (a GIF has at most 256 per frame) and the
    number of frames. KVGrainy tunes all three, and the <a href="/make-gif-smaller/">Fine-Tune GIF</a> tab lets you decide which
    to give up first. A GIF or clip comes out as a GIF, or as an animated WebP if you choose WebP, which is usually much smaller.
  </p>

  <h2>When it cannot fit</h2>
  <p>
    If even the smallest version is over your limit, KVGrainy says so and offers the smallest file it could make, clearly marked
    as over the limit, so you are never left with nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>The same code runs in the desktop app</h2>
  <p>
    The search is written once, in Rust, and runs in this page as WebAssembly. The desktop app runs the same code natively, with
    faster encoders and every CPU core, so it finishes sooner and can also make the reduced-colour PNGs. It is free and open
    source (AGPL-3.0): <a href={SITE.repo}>read the code</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it will not do</h2>
  <p>
    It is not an editor: it will not crop, recolour or retouch. It does not guess what is important in a picture. And it cannot
    make a detailed 12-megapixel photo fit under 50 KB without making it much smaller; the guides for
    <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb/">100 KB</a>, <a href="/compress-image-to-1mb/">1 MB</a> and
    <a href="/compress-image-to-2mb/">2 MB</a> show what that looks like on real photos.
  </p>
</Page>
